InferenceResponse(text='Improved Prompt\n\nGoal\nAssess the completeness and quality of a given table description and provide a structured, actionable score and guidance for improvement.\n\nInput\n- table_description: a text description of a database table. Provided as {table_description}. If the description is missing or empty, treat it as incomplete.\n\nOutput format\nReturn exactly three fields in this order:\n1) Score: [0-10]\n2) Reasoning: [concise justification for the score, explicitly noting which required pieces are present or missing]\n3) Suggestions: [bulleted list of concrete improvements to reach a higher score]\n\nScoring rubric (use as guidance to determine the score)\n- 0-2: Major gaps; missing table name, no column-level details, no data types, no business context\n- 3-5: Some details present but many gaps (e.g., missing data types, missing keys/constraints, weak business context)\n- 6-7: Most columns described with names and meanings; data types present; some missing items (e.g., nullability, constraints, sample values, or context)\n- 8-9: All required pieces present: table name, purpose, every column with name and meaningful description, data type for each column, nullability, keys/constraints (PK, FK, UK), sample values, business context and typical usage, relationships to other tables or data sources\n- 10: All of the above plus optional enhancements: data lineage, update frequency, data source, enumerations/domains, governance notes, and data quality caveats\n\nWhat to include in a high-quality description (to earn 8+)\n- Table name and one-line purpose\n- For each column, provide:\n  - Column name\n  - Meaning/description\n  - Data type (with length/precision if applicable)\n  - Nullability (NOT NULL / NULL)\n  - Key and constraint information (PK, FK, UK, unique constraints, default values)\n  - Example value(s) (at least one example per column)\n- Relationships: foreign keys to other tables or external data sources\n- Business context: who uses this table, typical use cases, and how it integrates with other data\n- Domain constraints: enumerations or allowed/value ranges, units of measure\n- Data quality and lineage: source system, update frequency, data freshness, notable caveats or issues\n\nFormatting guidelines\n- Use clear headings or bullet points for each section and each column\n- Use consistent data-type notation (e.g., INT, VARCHAR(255), DECIMAL(10,2), DATE)\n- If a detail is unknown, mark it as UNKNOWN or TBD rather than guessing\n\nExamples (recommended)\n- Include one small, complete example description for a mock table to illustrate the expected level of detail and formatting\n- The example is for guidance only and should not be included in every response unless you are asked for it\n\nNotes\n- Do not add or infer information beyond what is provided in table_description; clearly indicate any uncertainties in Reasoning and Suggestions\n- The output should be concise and directly address how to improve the given description to reach a higher score.', usage=ResponseUsage(input_tokens=218, input_tokens_details=InputTokensDetails(cached_tokens=0), output_tokens=3526, output_tokens_details=OutputTokensDetails(reasoning_tokens=2880), total_tokens=3744), finish_reason=None, latency=19.38233232498169, cost=0.21809999999999996)